// How a program finds the files that come with it — a font, a config, a
// folder of data beside it: exe_path, its own file's path.
//
// main's args[0] is no good for it: it's what started the program
// wrote, and that may be just its name (found on the PATH), a path from
// another directory, or a link's. exe_path asks the system: the
// program's file, its path whole and as the system has it (real_path's: its
// letters' case as on disk, a link followed to the file itself), however
// it was started — so the files beside it are the ones beside the program's
// own file, not beside a link to it. get_dir gives its directory: the
// path up to its last separator, the separator kept, for a file's name to
// go after. Its separators are '/', on Windows too (which takes either), so
// a path is joined with "/" everywhere.
//
// It gives back a Path: up to 512 characters, its room past the path there
// to add to; get_dir gives one too, the directory, for a file's name to go
// after. Too_Large when the path is longer.
module exe_path

use mara.core
use mara.os
use mara.memory

#allocator Arena_Basic(1 * MB)

main :: fun() {
	storage : [64 * KB]byte

	path, e := exe_path()
	if e {
		print("couldn't tell:", e)
		return
	}

	// Its directory, the separator at its end.
	dir := get_dir(path)
	print("this program's directory ends with a separator:", dir.len > 0)

	// A file beside it: the directory, then the file's name.
	&dir + "exe_path.mara"
	_, re := read_entire_file(dir, &storage)
	print("its source beside it reads:", not re)
}
